  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Aerospace recorded $1.1B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date, across 201 awards with an average award size of $5.5M. Obligations fell down 20.8% year over year. The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer, obligating $940M, followed by the National Aeronautics and Space Administration at $114M and the Department of Energy at $36.8M. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) is the largest market with $1.1B of obligations. Historical obligations trended from $1.2B in FY21 and FY22, moved to $1.3B in FY23 and FY24, rose to $1.4B in FY25, and are $1.1B in FY26 to date. Several markets appear on the FY2026 list with no obligations reported or with negative obligations values. The top buying agencies remain concentrated in defense and civil space sectors, while smaller civil agencies account for the remaining obligations.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are Aerospace’s main federal customers?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is Aerospace’s largest customer, obligating $940M in FY2026 to date. The National Aeronautics and Space Administration is the second-largest buyer at $114M, followed by the Department of Energy at $36.8M. The Department of Commerce obligated $16.1M, the Department of the Interior obligated $3.3M, and the Department of Transportation obligated $2.5M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What are Aerospace’s top markets and recent trends?",
        "body": "Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) is the top market at $1.1B in FY2026 to date. The ranked markets include entries that show no FY2026 obligations reported or negative obligations values. Obligations increased from $1.2B in FY21 and FY22 to $1.4B in FY25, then moved to $1.1B in FY26 to date."
      }
    ]
  },
